# Line endings decided by what a file IS, not by each contributor's git config.
#
# git ls-files --eol in this repository reports i/lf w/crlf for every template: the index holds
# LF, and a working tree cloned with core.autocrlf=true (the Windows default) gets CRLF. Most of
# the time that is cosmetic. Here it is not: these templates are rendered into things a Linux
# machine executes. The boot script and the hook fragments become user_data, and the seeding
# script becomes a staging instance's user_data. A renderer that forgets to strip the CR ships
# "#!/bin/bash\r" or "command\r" to an instance that is already rented.
#
# Pinning eol=lf makes the worktree match the index for the files where byte-exactness matters.
# The renderers still call replace("\r\n", "\n") as well, on purpose: a file added before this
# existed, or written by a tool that emits CRLF, should not have to depend on git's configuration
# to produce a bootable script.
* text=auto

*.tf        text eol=lf
*.tfvars    text eol=lf
*.tfvars.json text eol=lf
*.tpl       text eol=lf
*.tftpl     text eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.hcl       text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.lock.hcl  text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf

# Checked out byte-for-byte.
*.png  binary
*.gz   binary
*.zip  binary
